Original forecast: {
  "strategy": "alt_strategy",
  "base_year": 2022,
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "forecast": [
    {
      "year": 2023,
      "revenue_cr": 72000,
      "net_income_cr": 17200,
      "eps": 13.95
    },
    {
      "year": 2024,
      "revenue_cr": 80500,
      "net_income_cr": 19300,
      "eps": 15.62
    },
    {
      "year": 2025,
      "revenue_cr": 89500,
      "net_income_cr": 21500,
      "eps": 17.4
    }
  ],
  "top_assumptions": [
    "Revenue growth normalizes toward ~11-12% per annum driven by FMCG (cigarettes + others), Agri Business recovery, and Hotels segment returning to profitability post-COVID; FY2022 showed a sharp 22.7% YoY jump (from 48151 to 59101 crores gross revenue) which is anomalous relative to the underlying 3-5 year CAGR of approximately 8-10%, with FY2021 being a depressed COVID base, so forward growth is moderated to ~11% blending mean-reversion and structural expansion.",
    "Net profit margin stabilizes around 24-25% (vs 25.5% in FY2022) as Hotels segment losses narrow further and FMCG-Others segment profitability improves, offset by higher input costs and capex-related depreciation from the ~2258 crore capex program; EPS growth tracks PAT growth with share count marginally increasing from ESOP issuances."
  ],
  "reasoning": "ITC's FY2022 gross revenue from sale of products and services (consolidated) was 59101 crores vs 48151 crores in FY2021, a 22.7% YoY jump \u2014 this is anomalous because FY2021 was a severely COVID-depressed base (Hotels segment revenue fell to 627 crores vs 1285 crores in FY2022; Agri Business surged 29% on commodity tailwinds). The 3-5 year CAGR is estimated at 8-10% normalizing for the pandemic distortion, so forward projections use an 11% blended rate (slightly above CAGR) acknowledging structural tailwinds: Hotels recovery still incomplete, FMCG-Others scaling up, Agri commodities elevated, and Paperboards expanding with significant capex (896 crores in FY2022). Balance sheet is very strong with negligible debt, RoE of 25%, and 37+ crores of unallocated corporate assets providing deployment capacity; capex of 2258 crores signals expansion confidence. Net profit margin is held at ~24% for FY2023-25 as Hotels profitability recovers (from -183 crore segment result in FY2022) and FMCG-Others EBIT improves, partially offset by higher depreciation from capex and normalizing investment income. Consolidated PAT of 15503 crores in FY2022 and basic EPS of 12.37 form the base; EPS projections assume ~1233 crore shares with marginal ESOP dilution."
}
Critique: {
  "company": "ITC",
  "base_year": 2022,
  "strategy": "alt_strategy",
  "assumptions": [
    {
      "assumption": "Revenue growth normalizes to ~11% per annum driven by FMCG, Agri Business recovery, and Hotels segment returning to profitability, with FY2022's 22.7% spike treated as a COVID-base anomaly.",
      "failure_scenario": "Cigarette volume growth stagnates or declines due to aggressive excise duty hikes in Union Budgets FY2024-25 and accelerating illicit trade substitution, while Agri Business revenues contract sharply if commodity price tailwinds reverse (e.g., wheat/soyabean prices normalise post-Ukraine war resolution), causing blended revenue growth to undershoot 11% materially \u2014 potentially falling to 6-7%, making the FY2025 revenue estimate of 89,500 crores overstated by 10-15%.",
      "risk": "High"
    },
    {
      "assumption": "Net profit margin stabilizes at 24-25%, supported by Hotels segment loss narrowing and FMCG-Others profitability improvement, with capex-related depreciation and input cost pressures only partially offsetting these gains.",
      "failure_scenario": "Persistent input cost inflation (leaf tobacco, wheat, packaging materials) combined with competitive pricing pressure in FMCG-Others (where ITC is a challenger brand against HUL, Nestle) forces margin sacrifice to defend market share; simultaneously, Hotels segment recovery is delayed by a discretionary spending slowdown in a high-interest-rate environment, keeping segment losses elevated and pushing consolidated PAT margins below 22%, implying net income 8-10% below forecast.",
      "risk": "Medium"
    },
    {
      "assumption": "EPS growth closely tracks PAT growth with only marginal share count dilution from ESOP issuances, anchored on a base of ~1,233 crore shares outstanding.",
      "failure_scenario": "ITC pursues a large debt-funded or equity-funded acquisition in FMCG or Hotels (consistent with its stated diversification strategy and strong balance sheet), resulting in significant equity dilution or a step-up in depreciation and finance costs that compresses EPS growth independently of revenue/PAT trends; alternatively, a large special dividend or buyback restructuring alters the per-share economics assumed in the model, rendering EPS projections mechanically unreliable.",
      "risk": "Low"
    }
  ],
  "confidence_score": 52,
  "overall_critique": "The forecast is methodologically sound in identifying the COVID-base anomaly and moderating forward growth, but it materially underweights regulatory and commodity-cycle risks that are central to ITC's two largest revenue segments \u2014 cigarettes and Agri Business \u2014 both of which are structurally volatile and policy-sensitive. The 11% blended revenue growth assumption and stable 24-25% margin assumption compound optimistically, leaving the FY2025 EPS estimate of 17.40 vulnerable to a 15-20% downside in an adverse but plausible macro and regulatory scenario."
}

Step 1: For each "High" risk assumption in the critique, adjust the corresponding forecast values by a conservative factor (reduce growth by 20-30% for that driver).
Step 2: For "Medium" risk assumptions, reduce growth by 10% for that driver.
Step 3: Leave "Low" risk assumptions unchanged.
Step 4: Calculate a confidence range: ± percentage based on the number of High (±15%) and Medium (±8%) risks.

RESPONSE FORMAT: Begin your response DIRECTLY with the opening brace { — no reasoning or text before the JSON.

Return ONLY valid JSON:
{
  "strategy": "<strategy>",
  "base_year": <YYYY>,
  "revised_forecast": [
    {"year": <YYYY>, "revenue_cr": <number>, "net_income_cr": <number>, "eps": <number>,
     "confidence_range_pct": <number>},
    ...
  ],
  "revisions_made": ["<description of each change>"],
  "final_confidence_score": <0-100>
}
